1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,000
A one percent fee can cost you over three hundred thousand dollars.

2
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:08,900
Here's the setup. You put five hundred dollars a month into an investment for forty years.

3
00:00:09,260 --> 00:00:12,220
That's two hundred forty thousand dollars of your own money.

4
00:00:12,680 --> 00:00:16,580
Let's say it grows seven percent a year. That's just an assumption, not a promise.

5
00:00:17,260 --> 00:00:22,300
Now picture two identical funds, but one charges a one percent fee every year.

6
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:24,720
Hit like if one percent sounds like nothing.

7
00:00:25,300 --> 00:00:29,820
A fee is what the fund keeps for managing your money. One percent sounds tiny, right?

8
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:32,520
After ten years, the gap is only about forty-six hundred dollars.

9
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:37,560
But here's the thing. The fee doesn't take one percent of what you add.

10
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:41,900
It takes one percent of everything, every year, growth included.

11
00:00:42,520 --> 00:00:44,680
Send this to the friend who's never checked their fees.

12
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:48,940
At forty years, the no-fee fund has about one point three million dollars.

13
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:51,420
The one percent fund? About nine hundred ninety-six thousand.

14
00:00:53,580 --> 00:00:56,120
Follow if you like knowing the math before the fine print.

15
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:58,220
That tiny fee cost you three hundred sixteen thousand dollars.

16
00:01:00,780 --> 00:01:03,040
That's more than all the money you ever put in.

17
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:05,900
Is one percent still small? Yes or no.

